content.text:
No stripANSI helper exists. lipgloss styles applied in tests — the rendered strings contain ANSI codes. Simplest: strip ANSI with a regex in the test, or set styles... Styles are package-level vars with lipgloss colors; in a test without a TTY, lipgloss may still emit codes. Write a small stripANSI regex helper in the test file:

var ansiRegex = regexp.MustCompile(`\x1b\[[0-9;]*[a-zA-Z]`)

Add it to the test file.

Data.Arguments:
{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'
s = open('internal/tui/render_test.go').read()
s = s.replace('''import (
	"strings"
	"testing"
)''', '''import (
	"regexp"
	"strings"
	"testing"
)

var ansiPattern = regexp.MustCompile(`\\x1b\\[[0-9;]*[a-zA-Z]`)

// stripANSI removes terminal styling so tests can assert on plain text.
func stripANSI(text string) string {
	return ansiPattern.ReplaceAllString(text, "")
}''')
open('internal/tui/render_test.go','w').write(s)
EOF
